course: dictionary
lesson: 9645
title: Many meanings of style
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word style has more than one meaning, so the sentence around it tells you which one to use. «Every family has its own style.» Here style means the particular way a family does things, not clothes.
explain: A style is a way of doing, making or saying something. «Singing styles differ around the world» means the ways people use their voices are different. Check with the swap test: put way in place of style. «Every family has its own way» still makes sense, so way is a good clue.
explain: Style can also mean fashion or design, as in a hairstyle. In «a London lad greets his mate with "Wagwan, fam!"», the style is his relaxed way of talking, not his clothes. Switching between styles is a skill, not being fake.
fact: Style is a noun: a way of doing, making or saying something, such as a style of writing, swimming or painting.
fact: Style can mean a fashion or design for clothes or hair, such as a hairstyle.
fact: Style can also mean being elegant or doing something in an impressive way.
fact: As a verb, to style hair or clothes means to arrange them in a certain way.
fact: Informal style is relaxed and chatty, and formal style is careful and polite. The style you choose is called your register.
exercise: «Every family has its own style.» What does style mean here? | A shop that sells clothes | A hairdresser's chair | *The particular way a family does things | A loud noise at home
exercise: «Everyone has at least two ways of talking: informal and formal.» Which word names a style that is relaxed and chatty? | register | formal | *informal | polite
exercise: Ella says style is only about clothes. Is she right? | *No, style can also mean a way of doing or saying something | Yes, style only means clothes | No, style means a shop | Yes, style only means hair
test: «Painting in the style of a famous artist» means what? | A shop that sells paint | *A particular way of painting, like the artist's own way | A price for a painting | A brush with a long handle
test: What does the verb to style mean? | To dye hair a new colour | To cut hair very short | *To arrange hair or clothes in a certain way | To wash hair
exam: The style you choose when talking to teachers and strangers is your register. What is the careful, polite style called? | informal | *formal | chatty | slang
